(5) Forms of participation of the community and the business sector in the implementation of child protection may include:
 a. provision of safe houses and shelters;
 b. establishment of integrated child service centers;
 c. establishment and management of child orphanages;
 d. establishment of rehabilitation centers for children victims of abuse of narcotics, psychotropics and other addictive substances (NAPZA);
 e. provision of assistance to children in conflict with the law;
 f. provision of educational scholarships;
 g. provision of health cost assistance;
 h. provision of Children's Playgrounds;
 i. active supervision of children's activities that are not in accordance with the norms prevailing in society;
 j. other forms of participation of the community and the business sector related to the implementation of child protection.

